//:: cases TC_LK_5_LockDeadWithoutInvUnsat
//:: tools silicon
//:: verdict Fail

// TC-LK-5: lockDead fires WITHOUT lockInvariantUnsatisfiable.
//
// The lock invariant (val > 0) is satisfiable in isolation, so
// CheckLockInvariantSatisfiability's isolated FramedProof:
//   Inhale(Perm(val,write) ** val>0); Refute(ff)
// does NOT fire LockInvariantUnsatisfiable — the invariant is fine.
//
// However, the method's precondition constrains a SEPARATE field (flag == 1),
// and an assume inside the method contradicts it (flag == 2).
// This makes the state dead BEFORE 'lock this' is reached.
// The per-site Refute(ff)(LockDeadCodeBlame) in EncodeIntrinsicLock
// catches the already-dead state at the lock: lockDead fires.
//
// Distinction from TC-LK-2:
//   TC-LK-2  →  lockInvariantUnsatisfiable + lockDead  (invariant itself is bad)
//   TC-LK-5  →  lockDead only               (invariant OK; context made state dead)
lock_invariant Perm(val, write) ** val > 0;
class LiveInvDeadContext {
    int val;
    int flag;

    requires committed(this) ** Perm(flag, write) ** flag == 1;
    void method() {
        assume flag == 2;
        // path condition: flag == 1 ∧ flag == 2 → false.
        // DetectDeadCode: deadBranch("code after assume flag == 2") fires.
        lock this;
        // lockDead fires: state was already dead before lock.
        // lockInvariantUnsatisfiable does NOT fire.
        unlock this;
        // unlockDead fires: dead state persists through unlock.
    }
}
